th  that I mean I don't have a problem with  that  especially because of his being burned  as a child and this gave him like this  insane fear of flame that he's had he's  been a certifiable killer and  just a cruel kind of man his whole life  for the most part and so in this world  isn't a bad thing when a character gets  to redeem themselves my interest lies in  the ability itself and the Brotherhood  without banners and their intentions the  reason why it stands out is because  we've seen in the trailers quite a bit  that these guys are going to be teamed  up with John at some point north of the  wall you know one of the biggest things  that we saw in the and the winners here  trailer was that dramatic shot of  barrack was his burning sword so that is  going to happen and we know from season  six that that was their intention when  they ran into the Hound in the first  place no way back in season three they  had Aria and Gendry and they end up  catching the Hound and instead of just  killing him they give him a trial by  combat where he fights beric Dondarrion  so in that short bit of time you know we  find out that they're still hanging out  because they're trying to kill his  brother you know there's that back and  forth that exchange where he says you  fight for ghosts because they say that  Ned had told them to go out and kill the  mountain and Ned and Robert have long  been dead at this point he find out that  they're really into the Lord of Light  now and Thoros has always been like he  arrives in King's Landing  for that reason more or less he was a  bad priest they sent him to King's  Landing to recruit people to make the  story really simple when he got there he  was more into the idea of drinking and  everything else so he didn't do a very  good job but either way he has visions  in the flame as well he sees things and  he's also been able to bring beric back  to life which we all know about that so  it's a little bit odd that they give him  a trial which he beats barrack and  single combat and then he's free to go  letting him go isn't we're at that point  because in their minds as religious  people the Lord of Light has spoken and  he's saying that the hounds all right so  my thought was this is their motivation  because they think or Thoreau's has seen  something that tells them that he is  important because if they if he had  and we know that the divisions aren't  exactly clear all the time  this could be a good reason to have him  fight Barrack who they know they can  bring back anyways most likely so they  set up this trial by combat the hound  comes out Thoreau springs back back to  life and that's their answer to whether  or not the hound is important in that  scenario what's also weird here is that  he ends up taking Aria after she runs  away and you know they were looking for  her initially but they sort of just  leave it go now all this stuff could be  inconsequential so I don't want to  ramble on about it but I do think it's  interesting that he had this vision we  can presume that Thoreau's has had  similar visions and that he may have  seen some bigger importance for the  hound which got them in the same place  now the whole idea whether the Lord of  Light real or is real or not is up for  debate I mean the magic is real we know  that because we've seen it we know  there's magic involved like these these  are things are actually happening but we  don't know if there's a supernatural  entity that's in charge or but something  else and then I went into all this in my  are the gods real series so I won't  rehash it now but it's something to  think about if you think about it we saw  in the trailers that there were going to  be up north we know we've seen Derek and  the Hound those fighting up there we can  imagine that they all make it there so  you figure there's like three Lord of  Light devotees that are going to be  fighting at John's side now how many of  them are actually going to live that's  the